<?xml version='1.0' encoding='UTF-8'?>
<preface xml:id="ck3-preface">
  <title>Preface</title>
  <p>Let <m>X\subset\mathbb P^5_{\mathbb C}</m> be a smooth cubic fourfold and let <m>h=c_1(\mathcal O_X(1))</m>. We have <m>h^{3,1}(X)=1</m>, and the Tate-twisted primitive Hodge structure <m>H^4(X,\mathbb Z)_{\mathrm{prim}}(1)</m> has Hodge numbers <m>(1,20,1)</m>. We study when additional algebraic classes on <m>X</m> give an association with a polarized K3 surface, and how this association relates to the geometry and rationality of <m>X</m>.</p>
  <p>The variety of lines <m>F(X)</m> is an irreducible holomorphic symplectic fourfold of <m>K3^{[2]}</m> deformation type, by Beauville and Donagi <xref ref="bib-ck3-beauville-donagi"/>. Their incidence correspondence identifies primitive cubic cohomology, after a Tate twist and a change of sign in the pairing, with primitive second cohomology of <m>F(X)</m> relative to its Plücker class. Kuznetsov's component
    <md number="no">\Ku(X)=\langle\mathcal O_X,\mathcal O_X(1),\mathcal O_X(2)\rangle^\perp\subset D^b(X)</md>
    is a 2-Calabi–Yau category (i.e., its Serre functor is isomorphic to <m>[2]</m>) <xref ref="bib-ck3-kuznetsov-cubics"/>. We also ask when <m>\Ku(X)\simeq D^b(S,\alpha)</m> for a projective K3 surface <m>S</m> and a Brauer class <m>\alpha\in\operatorname{Br}(S)</m>, and when such an equivalence can be chosen with <m>\alpha=0</m>.</p>
  <p>By Voisin's Torelli theorem, the integral Hodge structure on <m>H^4(X,\mathbb Z)</m>, together with the cup-product pairing and the distinguished class <m>h^2</m>, determines <m>X</m> up to isomorphism <xref ref="bib-ck3-voisin-torelli"/>, <xref ref="bib-ck3-voisin-torelli-correction"/>. Laza <xref ref="bib-ck3-laza-period"/> and Looijenga <xref ref="bib-ck3-looijenga-period"/> independently described the image of the period map by excluding two arithmetic hyperplane arrangements.</p>
  <p>Hassett's loci <m>\mathcal C_d</m> consist of cubics admitting a saturated rank-two lattice of algebraic classes containing <m>h^2</m>, with discriminant <m>d</m>. They are nonempty divisors exactly when <m>d&gt;6</m> and <m>d\equiv0,2\pmod6</m>. His association theorem determines when the orthogonal complement of a specified label comes from the primitive cohomology of a polarized K3 surface <xref ref="bib-ck3-hassett-special"/>. Comparisons of integral Mukai lattices <xref ref="bib-ck3-addington-thomas"/>, <xref ref="bib-ck3-huybrechts-category"/> and moduli of stable objects <xref ref="bib-ck3-blmnps"/> give criteria for these derived equivalences. These notes explain the comparisons and their arithmetic conditions, then study the geometric rationality proofs for <m>\mathcal C_{14},\mathcal C_{26},\mathcal C_{38},\mathcal C_{42}</m> and the quadric-fibration criterion for cubics containing a plane.</p>
  <p>The intended reader knows complex projective algebraic geometry, intersection theory, Hodge structures, bounded derived categories, and integral lattice theory. Residue and Chern-class calculations, mutations, and residual-intersection arguments are given in detail. The proofs of Torelli and of the period image are explained through their principal reductions. Results from holomorphic symplectic geometry, degeneration theory, homological projective duality, and stability theory are stated in the forms used here, with their hypotheses and references.</p>
  <p><xref ref="ck3-geometry"/> establishes the cohomological conventions. <xref ref="ck3-lines"/> constructs <m>F(X)</m> and its polarization, which enter the Torelli proof in <xref ref="ck3-torelli"/>. The period image in <xref ref="ck3-period-image"/> supplies the existence argument for special cubics and K3 associations in <xref ref="ck3-hassett"/>.</p>
  <p><xref ref="ck3-kuznetsov-category"/> constructs <m>\Ku(X)</m> and its geometric examples. <xref ref="ck3-dictionary"/> combines this category with the Hassett conditions through the Mukai lattice and moduli spaces, then studies stability spaces, autoequivalences, and Fourier–Mukai partners. The geometric continuation from <xref ref="ck3-hassett"/> to <xref ref="ck3-rationality"/> gives the birational constructions and specialization arguments, followed by recent necessary K3 conditions for rationality and a preprint on irrationality with geometric K3 categories. <xref ref="ck3-further-problems"/> brings together questions about Hassett intersections, stability spaces, categorical equivalence, L-equivalence, and rationality. <xref ref="ck3-category-appendix"/> contains the mutation and Serre-functor calculations.</p>
  <image xml:id="ck3-structure-map" source="images/ck3-structure-map.svg" width="100%">
    <description><p>Chapter dependencies. The main geometric branch is vertical; the right branch joins it in the Mukai lattice and derived equivalences. Further problems use the Hassett, categorical, and rationality results.</p></description>
  </image>
  <p>Solid arrows indicate main inputs; dashed arrows indicate local or secondary inputs.</p>
  <p>For rationality obstructions and further work on cubic fourfolds, see the companion <url href="../cubic-fourfolds/cubic-fourfold-notes.html">Notes on rationality of cubic fourfolds</url>. For further reading, Huybrechts' monograph <xref ref="bib-ck3-huybrechts-cubics"/> treats cubic hypersurfaces more broadly, Hassett's survey <xref ref="bib-ck3-hassett-survey"/> develops the connections with rationality, and Huybrechts' update <xref ref="bib-ck3-huybrechts-update"/> explains these derived equivalences and their consequences.</p>
  <p>I plan to keep updating these notes to correct errors, improve explanations, and add useful material. Corrections, questions, and suggestions are welcome.</p>
  <p>AI tools assisted in comparing sources, drafting explanations, checking calculations, editing the English, and preparing the PreTeXt source.</p>
</preface>
